## Why this exists

Most SaaS support inboxes get a flood of tickets, but only ~5% are actually urgent — fraud reports, production outages, suspicious logins. Today, that 5% gets surfaced by a human scanning the queue, which adds 15–60 minutes of latency. PagerDuty-style integrations exist, but they trigger off infrastructure metrics, not the *content* of customer messages. This demo classifies every incoming ticket and pipes the urgent ones into Slack so engineering and security see them in seconds, not after the next queue scan.

The bigger story is architectural. Most "AI automation" demos let the LLM do everything — classify, route, *and* fire side-effects — which makes the system non-deterministic and untestable. This system splits those jobs by design. The LLM classifies (categorical labels with confidence). A deterministic rules engine routes. A separate delivery layer fires the external action. Each boundary is enforced by the type system, not just a system prompt.

## What's built

- **AI classifier.** OpenAI gpt-4o-mini with `response_format: json_object` and temperature 0.1. Output is Zod-validated; any failure (network, malformed JSON, schema mismatch) drops to a safe `FALLBACK_CLASSIFICATION` so the rules engine never sees garbage.
- **Rules engine.** Pure TypeScript, ordered rule list. Six rules plus a fallback. Fraud sits above the high-priority short-circuit on purpose — a fraud + high-priority case still goes to the fraud team, not generic escalation.
- **Slack delivery layer.** When the rules engine routes to `fraud` or `escalation`, the pipeline posts a Block Kit message to a configured Slack webhook with category, priority, intent, confidence, the original input quote, and a link back to the dashboard. Other routes stay queue-only — the goal is high-signal alerts, not generic noise. Failures (Slack down, webhook revoked) are appended to the workflow's action log without breaking the rest of the pipeline.
- **Synthetic SaaS dataset.** A seeded faker generator (`mulberry32(1337)`) produces 600 historical workflows with realistic shape — time-of-day weighting in business hours, mixed categories (technical 35% / billing 30% / general 20% / fraud 5% / noise 5%), a believable status distribution (~92% completed, 5% failed with realistic error messages, 3% in-progress so the dashboard filters have data), and synthetic Slack delivery outcomes on the fraud/escalation rows.
- **REST API.** `POST /api/ingest`, `GET /api/workflows` (with `status`, `routedTo`, `category` filters via Postgres jsonb path queries), `GET /api/workflows/[id]`, `GET /api/insights`. Each route is a thin Next 16 route handler over a shared pipeline function.
- **Dashboard.** Sticky sidebar with status filters, four KPIs (total / success rate / avg processing time / queue depth), an AI insights panel that narrates pre-aggregated metrics, an interactive ingest form that runs the full classify → route → deliver pipeline live, and an expandable workflow table showing classification, matched rule, action log, and Slack delivery status per row.

## Technical choices worth calling out

### "AI classifies, rules route, delivery layer fires" — enforced by types

The rules engine takes a typed `Classification` and returns a typed `RoutingDecision`. The Slack delivery layer takes that `RoutingDecision` and returns a typed `ActionEntry`. There is no codepath where the LLM produces a routing target or fires an external call. The system prompt also enforces the boundary in language ("classify only — do NOT route, respond, or solve") but the type system is the actual guarantee.

### Validate at the system boundary, fall back inside

The classifier exposes `parseClassification(raw)` as a separate pure function — no API call, no side-effects, just JSON.parse + Zod. The smoke suite drives seven malformed inputs through it directly to assert each one falls through to the safe fallback. The Slack delivery layer applies the same pattern: a non-2xx response or thrown fetch becomes a `slack_failed` action entry on the workflow record, and the workflow itself still completes.

### Workflow record IS the audit trail

No separate event log table. Each workflow row carries its full lifecycle in one record: input, classification, routing decision, matched rule, action log (including Slack delivery outcome), status, durations, error message. The dashboard reads it as-is.

### Deterministic seed → testable engine

The seed is byte-identical across runs (`mulberry32(1337)` + `faker.seed(1337)`), so the smoke script can assert exact rule-ordering invariants: nine cases including "fraud beats high priority" and "billing+refund routes to finance, not just billing." Replacing the engine with a different ruleset is a one-file swap that the same assertions guard.

### Per-app Prisma client

Both this demo and the analytics dashboard run Prisma in the same npm workspace. Each generates its client into its own `prisma/generated/client/` directory so the hoisted `@prisma/client` doesn't get clobbered by whichever app generated last.

## Stack

Next.js 16 App Router · MUI v9 · Prisma + Neon Postgres · OpenAI gpt-4o-mini · Zod for I/O validation · Slack incoming webhooks for the delivery layer · `@faker-js/faker` for the seed · TypeScript end-to-end.
